Optimized Linux File System Layout – 50 GB
/ (root): 10 GB – OS, applications and essential system files.
/var: 15 GB – logs, caches and variable data.
/home: 15 GB – user files.
/tmp: 5 GB – temporary files.
Swap: 5 GB – memory support.
Log management

Use logrotate to prevent logs from consuming the disk.

Example:

/var/log/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    size 1G
    missingok
    notifempty
}
Keep /var separate so log growth cannot fill the root filesystem.
Set log rotation and compression.
Use df -h and du -sh to monitor disk usage.
Configure alerts when /var reaches about 80–85%.
Reserve free space for critical services.

Result: Logs are controlled below the 10 GB requirement, while separate partitions prevent one type of data from exhausting the entire 50 GB disk.

02. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads

The main problem is excessive context switching, which wastes CPU time.

Optimization techniques

Tune the scheduler

Use nice and renice to control process priority.
nice -n -5 ./program
renice -n -5 -p PID

CPU affinity

Bind important real-time processes to selected CPUs.
taskset -c 2,3 ./program

Real-time scheduling

Use SCHED_FIFO or SCHED_RR for time-critical tasks.
chrt -f 80 ./program
Reduce unnecessary kernel activity
Disable unused services and unnecessary kernel modules.
Kernel modules
Load only required modules using modprobe.
Remove unused modules using modprobe -r.

Monitor context switches

vmstat 1
pidstat -w 1

Result: Proper scheduler tuning, CPU affinity and real-time priorities reduce unnecessary context switching and improve response time without requiring hardware upgrades.

03. Processing 1 Million Log Entries Efficiently

For 1 million entries, avoid slow shell loops such as while read. Use optimized commands that process data internally.

Example
#!/bin/bash
grep "ERROR" app.log | sort | uniq -c > error_count.txt
awk '{print $1}' app.log | sort | uniq -c > status_count.txt

For multiple operations, awk is especially efficient:

awk '/ERROR/{e++} /WARNING/{w++} END{print "Errors:",e,"Warnings:",w}' app.log
Commands used
grep – fast pattern searching.
awk – processes records in a single pass.
sort – efficient sorting of large datasets.
uniq – removes/counts duplicate entries.
Pipelines (|) – avoid unnecessary intermediate files.

For very large logs, process compressed logs with suitable tools and avoid repeatedly scanning the same file.

Result: Using awk, grep, and pipelines minimizes process creation and disk I/O, making processing of 1 million entries within 2 minutes achievable on suitable hardware.

04. Managing a Faulty Process Consuming 90% CPU

The system should not be restarted because critical services must remain available.

Step 1 – Identify the process
top

or:

ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head

Find the process with approximately 90% CPU usage and note its PID.

Step 2 – Monitor it
top -p PID

or:

pidstat -p PID 1
Step 3 – Reduce its priority
renice 10 -p PID

This allows critical processes to receive CPU time first.

Step 4 – Stop or restart the faulty process

Try graceful termination:

kill PID

If it does not respond:

kill -9 PID

If it is a system service:

systemctl restart service_name
Step 5 – Verify
top
systemctl status service_name

Result: The faulty process can be identified, monitored and controlled without rebooting the server, while critical services continue running.

05. RAM Allocation for 5 Virtual Machines
Given
Total RAM = 16 GB
Host OS requirement = 4 GB
Available for VMs = 12 GB
Number of VMs = 5
Minimum per VM = 2 GB

Required VM RAM:

5 × 2 GB = 10 GB

Therefore:

16 − 4 − 10 = 2 GB remaining

Proposed allocation
Resource	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM1	2 GB
VM2	2 GB
VM3	2 GB
VM4	2 GB
VM5	2 GB
Remaining	2 GB
Total	16 GB
Efficient strategy
Allocate 2 GB minimum to each VM.
Keep 4 GB reserved for the host.
Use the remaining 2 GB as host/virtualization overhead reserve.
Use memory ballooning if supported.
Avoid assigning more RAM than required.
Monitor RAM using virtualization tools.
If workloads vary, dynamically adjust VM memory.

Result: All 5 VMs meet their 2 GB minimum requirement, the host retains its required 4 GB, and 2 GB remains available as a safety/overhead reserve.
